Mutex locks (spinlocks)
A mutex lock is the simplest software tool built on the hardware primitives: a boolean available ; acquire() busy-waits until
the lock is free and takes it; release() frees it. Both must be atomic (often via compare-and-swap). Drawback: busy waiting
wastes CPU cycles — this kind of lock is called a spinlock (acceptable when the lock is held for a very short time, and on
multiprocessors a spinlock avoids a context switch).


