Producer–Consumer (Bounded-Buffer) problem

 A producer generates items and puts them in a buffer of n slots; a consumer removes items. The producer must wait when the
 buffer is full, the consumer when it is empty, and access to the buffer must be mutually exclusive.


 semaphore mutex = 1; /* mutual exclusion on buffer */
 semaphore empty = n; /* counts EMPTY slots */
 semaphore full = 0; /* counts FULL slots */

 Producer() { Consumer() {
 while (true) { while (true) {
 produce_item(x); wait(full); /* any item? */
 wait(empty); /* free slot? */ wait(mutex);
 wait(mutex); remove(x);
 insert(x); signal(mutex);
 signal(mutex); signal(empty); /* slot freed */
 signal(full); /* item added */ consume_item(x);
 } }
 } }



 Why the order of waits is important: Order matters: wait(empty) / wait(full) before wait(mutex) . If the producer
 did wait(mutex) first and then found the buffer full, it would sleep holding the mutex, the consumer could never enter →
 deadlock.




